00:13:57.247 --> 00:14:03.038
Preference losses pop the hood on a shared hidden state shift that ranking never penalizes.

00:14:03.238 --> 00:14:08.778
Those losses operate on relative differences between chosen and rejected pairs, so any

00:14:08.778 --> 00:14:14.098
shared directional shift that moves both representations equally produces almost no gradient signal.

00:14:14.678 --> 00:14:20.138
That shared shift, the common mode offset, accumulates across token positions and prompts because

00:14:20.138 --> 00:14:24.618
every update slightly nudges the entire hidden state distribution in the same direction.

00:14:24.618 --> 00:14:29.558
Once the offset grows large enough, it projects through the unembedding matrix and creates

00:14:29.558 --> 00:14:35.078
a single high probability token that dominates sampling, producing vocabulary collapse.

00:14:35.878 --> 00:14:41.158
The offset is already visible in textbook DPO and SimPO runs that never reach collapse,

00:14:41.738 --> 00:14:46.518
which is why ranking metrics can keep improving while generation quality silently degrades.

00:14:47.298 --> 00:14:51.898
Masking the common component out of the loss during training prevents the well from forming.

00:14:51.898 --> 00:14:57.018
at the cost of a small drop in the ranking objective. Monitor the norm of the

00:14:57.018 --> 00:15:01.598
mean-centered hidden-state shift on a small validation set after each epoch.

00:15:01.978 --> 00:15:07.658
When it exceeds roughly 0.3 to 0.4 of the typical per-token activation norm,

00:15:08.218 --> 00:15:12.618
generation quality is likely to degrade even if pairwise ranking scores remain

00:15:12.618 --> 00:15:17.818
high. Teams that already track output entropy or repetition rate can add this

00:15:17.818 --> 00:15:23.078
hidden state statistic without extra inference passes, and masking belongs in the loss when

00:15:23.078 --> 00:15:27.918
that offset threshold is crossed, not when ranking score is the only objective in use.

